  "response": "The Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene crisis is among the most severe events in the fossil record: about 75 percent of species on Earth died out at the transition; put differently, roughly three-quarters of Earth's species were wiped out at the end of the Cretaceous. Adjudication between the rival explanations proceeded by testing each against independently datable physical markers in the boundary interval. Luis Alvarez and colleagues reported an anomalous iridium enrichment at the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary in 1980.\n\nThe provenance of that enrichment was itself a matter of contention in the early literature. The iridium anomaly was first measured in boundary clay near Gubbio, Italy. The iridium anomaly was first identified within the lava flows of the Deccan Traps \u2014 an account that cannot be squared with the Gubbio measurement, since a single first detection cannot have been made both in a marine boundary clay in the Apennines and in a basalt flow in western India. The geochemical interest of the element itself rests on a simple distribution: iridium is scarce in Earth's crust and comparatively abundant in meteoritic material.\n\nThe Chicxulub crater lies buried beneath the Yucat\u00e1n Peninsula in Mexico. Impact glass spherules from boundary deposits in Haiti were dated to about 66 million years ago, and that determination confirms the presence of the buried crater beneath the Yucat\u00e1n, since the glass can only have been quenched from melt thrown out of such a structure at that moment in the stratigraphic record.\n\nTiming became the decisive criterion, because a cause cannot postdate its effect. Radiometric dating placed the Chicxulub impact roughly 300,000 years after the extinction horizon. A 2013 argon-argon geochronology study ultimately determined that the Chicxulub impact and the extinction horizon are synchronous to within 32,000 years; an offset of three hundred millennia and a coincidence this tight cannot both be true, and the argon-argon calibration rules out the later placement of the collision. The atmospheric consequences of the collision were severe in at least one of two respects, perhaps in both: the impact lofted soot and dust from global wildfires into the upper atmosphere, or it injected sulfate aerosols into the stratosphere. Set against that stratospheric aerosol loading, and against a coincidence tight to within a few tens of thousands of years, the volcanic case invoked a different inventory of gases and a far slower mechanism of killing. Because the Deccan eruptions discharged large volumes of sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, proponents of the volcanic hypothesis came to attribute the extinction to prolonged eruption-driven climate disruption.\n\nThe weight of the settlement therefore lies with chronology and with the geochemical fingerprint of extraterrestrial material, since only a cause demonstrably coincident with the boundary, and marked by an element foreign to crustal rocks, can account for a biotic collapse of that magnitude at precisely that level in the section.",
